\begin{abstract}
We develop an exact combinatorial and information-theoretic theory of binary
patterns on a finite cell set that are invariant under a group of symmetries,
with the discrete time--pitch torus $\ZZ_p \times \ZZ_q$ as the running model.
The foundation is the \emph{orbit capacity theorem}: if a group $G$ acts on a
finite cell set with $m$ orbits, then the space of $G$-invariant binary patterns
has cardinality exactly $2^m$, equivalently capacity exactly $m$ bits. Combining
this with Burnside's lemma converts the capacity into an average of fixed-point
counts, and we evaluate it in closed form for the four generators of a planar
crystallographic group acting on a torus.

Our main quantitative results are: the point-reflection
(retrograde--inversion) capacity $2^{(pq + \tau(p)\tau(q))/2}$, where
$\tau(n)\in\{1,2\}$ counts the elements of $\ZZ_n$ equal to their own negatives;
the full-translation capacity $2^{q}$; the quarter-turn capacity
$2^{(p^2+2\tau(p)+\tau(p)^2)/4}$ on the square torus, specializing to
$2^{(p^2+3)/4}$ for odd $p$ and $2^{(p^2+8)/4}$ for even $p$; and the glide
capacity $2^{pq/2}$ for even $p$, obtained from the fixed-point freeness of the
glide reflection.

Structurally we prove a \emph{descent dichotomy} determining exactly which
elements of the order-eight point group of the square lattice survive
quotienting to a $p\times q$ torus (all of them iff $p=q$; otherwise exactly the
order-four diagonal subgroup); \emph{strict} antitonicity of capacity in the
symmetry group together with its exact converse; and a \emph{realizability
theorem} showing that every subgroup of the ambient group occurs precisely as
the symmetry group of an explicit pattern.

We formalize the predicate ``canon at time distance $g$'' and determine its
complete numerical signature: $k$ is the onset count of some canon at distance
$g$ on the $p\times q$ torus if and only if $d \mid k$ and $k \le pq$, where $d$
is the additive order of $g$. Finally we upgrade the count to a probabilistic
statement: every distribution on invariant patterns has Shannon entropy at most
$m$ bits, with equality exactly for the uniform distribution, and the natural
biased model --- switch each orbit on independently with probability $\theta$
--- has entropy exactly $m\,H_2(\theta)$, so its entropy deficit is exactly
$m(1-H_2(\theta))$: stylistic bias costs a fixed number of bits per orbit.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}

\subsection{The counting problem}

Let $\alpha$ be a finite set of \emph{cells} and let a group $G$ act on
$\alpha$. A \emph{binary pattern} is a function $f : \alpha \to \{0,1\}$;
without constraints there are $2^{|\alpha|}$ of them. The pattern is
\emph{$G$-invariant} when $f(g\cdot a) = f(a)$ for all $g\in G$, $a\in\alpha$.

The motivating instance is a quantized musical grid: $\alpha$ is a discrete
time--pitch (or time--instrument) grid, a cell is a possible onset, and $G$ is a
group of musical transformations --- time shifts, inversion, retrograde,
retrograde--inversion, glide reflections. The question ``how much room does a
style leave?'' becomes ``how many patterns are invariant under the style's
symmetry group?'', and the question ``how much information does a rhythm
carry?'' becomes a Shannon-entropy question over that space. This paper answers
both, exactly, for the natural toroidal models.

\subsection{Summary of contributions}

\begin{enumerate}
\item \textbf{Orbit capacity} (\S\ref{sec:capacity}): invariant patterns are in
  bijection with Boolean functions on the orbit set, so their number is $2^m$.
\item \textbf{Burnside evaluation} (\S\ref{sec:burnside}), in division-free and
  averaged forms.
\item \textbf{Closed-form capacities on the torus} (\S\ref{sec:translations}--%
  \S\ref{sec:glide}) for translations, point reflection, quarter turns, glides.
\item \textbf{Descent dichotomy} (\S\ref{sec:descent}) for the point group of
  the square lattice on a rectangular torus.
\item \textbf{Strict monotonicity and its converse} (\S\ref{sec:monotone}).
\item \textbf{Realizability of every symmetry type} (\S\ref{sec:realize}).
\item \textbf{The complete onset spectrum of canons} (\S\ref{sec:canon}).
\item \textbf{Entropy theory} (\S\ref{sec:entropy}): the maximum-entropy bound
  with equality case, and the exact per-orbit entropy deficit of the
  orbit-Bernoulli model.
\end{enumerate}

\subsection{What is not claimed}

We deliberately do not assert a ``seventeen wallpaper groups of rhythm''
classification. Section~\ref{sec:descent} shows why such a claim requires care:
on a non-square torus, half of the point group of the plane fails to descend at
all. Similarly, we treat musical labels (``canon'', ``round'') as predicates to
be tested, not as outputs of a classification.

\section{Setup and notation}

Throughout $G$ is a group acting on a set $\alpha$ of cells; $\alpha$ is finite
whenever a cardinality is asserted.

\begin{definition}[Orbit relation]
Cells $a,b\in\alpha$ are equivalent, $a\sim_G b$, if $b = g\cdot a$ for some
$g\in G$. The classes are the \emph{orbits}; the orbit set is $\alpha/G$ and
$m := |\alpha/G|$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Invariant pattern]
A \emph{$G$-invariant pattern} is a function $f:\alpha\to\{0,1\}$ constant on
orbits. We write $\Pat_G(\alpha)$ for the set of these.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}[Pointwise form of invariance]\label{lem:pointwise}
A pattern $f$ is constant on orbits if and only if $f(g\cdot a) = f(a)$ for all
$g\in G$, $a\in\alpha$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
If $f$ is constant on orbits then $g\cdot a \sim_G a$ gives the pointwise
identity. Conversely, if $a\sim_G b$ then $a = g\cdot b$ for some $g$, whence
$f(a) = f(g\cdot b) = f(b)$.
\end{proof}

Lemma~\ref{lem:pointwise} is a bijection of descriptions: the two formulations
define literally the same set of patterns.

\begin{definition}[Fixed set]
For $g\in G$, $\Fix(g) := \{a\in\alpha : g\cdot a = a\}$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Two-torsion count]\label{def:tau}
For $n\ge 1$,
\[
  \tau(n) := \bigl|\{x\in\ZZ_n : -x = x\}\bigr|
  = \begin{cases} 2, & n \text{ even},\\ 1, & n \text{ odd}.\end{cases}
\]
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:tau}
Definition~\ref{def:tau} is correct: in $\ZZ_n$ with $n$ odd, $-x = x$ forces
$x=0$; with $n = 2m$, $m>0$, the solutions are exactly $x\in\{0,m\}$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
$-x = x$ is equivalent to $2x = 0$. If $n$ is odd then $2$ is coprime to $n$,
hence a unit, so $x=0$. If $n=2m$, lift $x$ to $v\in\{0,\dots,2m-1\}$; then
$2m \mid 2v$, so $m\mid v$, so $v\in\{0,m\}$; and $0\ne m$ in $\ZZ_{2m}$ since
$2m\nmid m$ for $m>0$.
\end{proof}

\begin{definition}[Time--pitch torus]
The $p\times q$ \emph{torus} is the cell set $\ZZ_p\times\ZZ_q$: time cyclic of
period $p$, pitch cyclic of period $q$. It has $pq$ cells.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Symmetry group of a pattern]\label{def:symgroup}
For $f:\alpha\to\{0,1\}$,
$\Sym_G(f) := \{g\in G : f(g\cdot a) = f(a) \text{ for all } a\}$.
\end{definition}

$\Sym_G(f)$ is a subgroup: it contains $1$; it is closed under products since
$f((gh)\cdot a) = f(g\cdot(h\cdot a)) = f(h\cdot a) = f(a)$; and closed under
inverses by applying the defining identity at $g^{-1}\cdot a$.

\begin{proposition}
$f$ is $G$-invariant if and only if $\Sym_G(f) = G$.
\end{proposition}

\section{The orbit capacity theorem}\label{sec:capacity}

\begin{theorem}[Orbit Capacity]\label{thm:capacity}
Let $G$ act on a finite cell set $\alpha$ with $m$ orbits. Then
$|\Pat_G(\alpha)| = 2^{m}$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
A pattern constant on orbits factors uniquely through the quotient map
$\alpha \twoheadrightarrow \alpha/G$; conversely any Boolean function on
$\alpha/G$ pulls back to an orbit-constant pattern. This is a bijection
$\Pat_G(\alpha)\cong\{0,1\}^{\alpha/G}$, and the right-hand side has $2^m$
elements.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Capacity in bits]
$\log_2|\Pat_G(\alpha)| = m$.
\end{corollary}

The entire counting problem is thereby reduced to counting orbits, and the
exponential shape means saving one orbit halves the pattern count. The trivial
group ($m = |\alpha|$) and a transitive group ($m=1$) are the limiting
instances. The bijection is used again in \S\ref{sec:entropy}: it is exactly the
parametrization along which we transport a product measure.

\section{Burnside evaluation of the capacity}\label{sec:burnside}

\begin{theorem}[Burnside's lemma]\label{thm:burnside}
For a finite group $G$ acting on a finite set $\alpha$,
$\sum_{g\in G}|\Fix(g)| = m\,|G|$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Count the incidence set $\{(g,a) : g\cdot a = a\}$ two ways: by $g$ it is
$\sum_g|\Fix(g)|$; by $a$ it is $\sum_a|\Stab(a)|$, which by orbit--stabilizer
equals $\sum_a |G|/|\Orb(a)| = |G|\,m$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Burnside capacity, division-free]
$|\Pat_G(\alpha)|^{|G|} = 2^{\sum_{g\in G}|\Fix(g)|}$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Raise Theorem~\ref{thm:capacity} to the power $|G|$ and substitute
Theorem~\ref{thm:burnside}.
\end{proof}

This form is an identity of natural numbers with no division and no hypothesis
that $|G|$ divides the fixed-point sum --- that divisibility is a consequence.

\begin{theorem}[Burnside capacity formula]\label{thm:burncap}
\[
  |\Pat_G(\alpha)| = 2^{\;\frac{1}{|G|}\sum_{g\in G}|\Fix(g)|}.
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{remark}[Algorithmic content]
Theorem~\ref{thm:burncap} is an algorithm. One need never enumerate orbits; it
suffices to solve, for each group element separately, the usually trivial
equation $g\cdot a = a$. All four capacities below are obtained this way.
\end{remark}

\section{Translations and point reflections}\label{sec:translations}

Let $C_p$ be the cyclic group of time shifts acting by $g\cdot(t,n)=(t+g,n)$.

\begin{lemma}[Freeness of the shift action]\label{lem:free}
For $g\ne 0$, $\Fix(g) = \emptyset$; and $\Fix(0)$ is everything.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
$(t+g,n) = (t,n)$ forces $g=0$ by cancellation in $\ZZ_p$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Ostinato capacity]
The number of patterns on the $p\times q$ torus invariant under every time shift
is exactly $2^q$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
By Lemma~\ref{lem:free} the Burnside sum is $pq$, and $|C_p| = p$, so
$m = q$. Geometrically the orbits are the $q$ horizontal circles.
\end{proof}

\begin{example}
On the $4\times 3$ grid there are exactly $8$ fully shift-invariant patterns,
out of $2^{12} = 4096$.
\end{example}

Now let $\{\pm 1\}$ act with $-1$ acting by $(t,n)\mapsto(-t,-n)$.

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:ptfix}
The fixed cells of the point reflection are the pairs $(t,n)$ with $-t=t$ and
$-n=n$; there are $\tau(p)\tau(q)$ of them.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
$-(t,n)=(t,n)$ holds coordinatewise, so the fixed set is the product of the two
two-torsion subgroups; apply Lemma~\ref{lem:tau} in each factor.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Point-reflection capacity]
The number of retrograde--inversion invariant patterns on the $p\times q$ torus
is exactly $2^{(pq+\tau(p)\tau(q))/2}$; for $p,q$ both odd this is
$2^{(pq+1)/2}$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Burnside sum $pq + \tau(p)\tau(q)$ over a group of order two; apply
Theorem~\ref{thm:burncap}.
\end{proof}

\begin{example}
$3\times 3$: $2^{5} = 32$. $4\times 3$: $2^{7} = 128$. The parity of the periods
is not cosmetic: even periods contribute additional two-torsion cells, hence
additional singleton orbits, hence strictly more capacity.
\end{example}

\section{Descent to a torus, and quarter-turn capacity}\label{sec:descent}

\subsection{Which planar symmetries descend?}

The $p\times q$ torus is the quotient of $\ZZ^2$ by
$\Lambda_{p,q} := p\ZZ\times q\ZZ$. A linear map descends to the torus exactly
when it preserves $\Lambda_{p,q}$. The linear parts of the isometries preserving
$\ZZ^2$ are the eight signed permutation matrices: \emph{diagonal} maps
$\sigma_{\varepsilon_1,\varepsilon_2}(v) = (\varepsilon_1 v_1,\varepsilon_2 v_2)$
and \emph{swapping} maps
$\rho_{\varepsilon_1,\varepsilon_2}(v) = (\varepsilon_1 v_2,\varepsilon_2 v_1)$,
$\varepsilon_i\in\{\pm 1\}$.

\begin{theorem}[Diagonal elements always descend]
For all $p,q$ and all units $\varepsilon_1,\varepsilon_2$, the map
$\sigma_{\varepsilon_1,\varepsilon_2}$ preserves $\Lambda_{p,q}$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
$p\mid v_1 \Rightarrow p\mid \varepsilon_1 v_1$, likewise in the second
coordinate.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Swapping elements descend only to square tori]
For units $\varepsilon_1,\varepsilon_2$, the map
$\rho_{\varepsilon_1,\varepsilon_2}$ preserves $\Lambda_{p,q}$ if and only if
$p = q$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
($\Leftarrow$) If $p=q$ the divisibility conditions are symmetric in the
coordinates. ($\Rightarrow$) Test on $(p,0)\in\Lambda_{p,q}$: its image is
$(0,\varepsilon_2 p)$, so $q\mid \varepsilon_2 p$, and as $\varepsilon_2$ is a
unit, $q\mid p$. Test on $(0,q)$: the image is $(\varepsilon_1 q, 0)$, so
$p\mid q$. Hence $p=q$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Descent dichotomy]\label{cor:dichotomy}
The subgroup of the order-eight point group $D_4$ descending to the $p\times q$
torus is all of $D_4$ when $p=q$, and exactly the order-four diagonal subgroup
$\{\sigma_{\pm 1,\pm 1}\}$ otherwise.
\end{corollary}

\begin{corollary}[Quarter-turn descent criterion]
The planar quarter turn $(t,n)\mapsto(-n,t)$ preserves $\Lambda_{p,q}$ --- that
is, descends to the $p\times q$ torus --- if and only if $p=q$.
\end{corollary}

This is the precise obstruction behind the remark that one cannot rotate a
rhythm by ninety degrees unless the time and pitch axes are commensurate:
rotating trades a beat for a semitone, and the trade is legal only when the two
periodicities coincide.

\subsection{The quarter-turn action on a square torus}

Write $\kappa(t,n) := (-n,t)$ on $\ZZ_p\times\ZZ_p$.

\begin{lemma}
$\kappa^2(t,n) = (-t,-n)$, $\kappa^3(t,n) = (n,-t)$, and
$\kappa^4 = \mathrm{id}$.
\end{lemma}

Thus $\kappa$ generates a cyclic group $Q$ of order dividing four, and
$\kappa^2$ is exactly the point reflection of \S\ref{sec:translations}: the two
constructions are compatible.

\begin{lemma}[Fixed cells of a quarter turn]\label{lem:qfix}
$\kappa(t,n) = (t,n)$ iff $n=t$ and $-t=t$. Hence
$|\Fix(\kappa)| = |\Fix(\kappa^3)| = \tau(p)$, the fixed cells being the
diagonal cells $(t,t)$ with $t$ of order at most two.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
The coordinate equations are $-n=t$ and $t=n$; substituting gives $-t=t$.
Lemma~\ref{lem:tau} counts the solutions. A map and its inverse have the same
fixed set, giving the count for $\kappa^3=\kappa^{-1}$.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}
$|\Fix(\kappa^2)| = \tau(p)^2$, by Lemma~\ref{lem:ptfix} with $q=p$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{theorem}[Burnside data for the quarter-turn action]\label{thm:qburn}
$\sum_{g\in Q}|\Fix(g)| = p^2 + 2\tau(p) + \tau(p)^2$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
The identity fixes all $p^2$ cells; $\kappa$ and $\kappa^3$ fix $\tau(p)$ each;
$\kappa^2$ fixes $\tau(p)^2$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Quarter-turn capacity]\label{thm:qcap}
The number of quarter-turn invariant patterns on the $p\times p$ torus is
exactly $2^{(p^2+2\tau(p)+\tau(p)^2)/4}$, and the action has exactly
$(p^2+2\tau(p)+\tau(p)^2)/4$ orbits.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Theorem~\ref{thm:burncap} with Theorem~\ref{thm:qburn} and $|Q|=4$; the orbit
count follows from injectivity of $m\mapsto 2^m$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Parity specializations]
\[
  |\Pat_Q| = \begin{cases}
    2^{(p^2+3)/4}, & p \text{ odd } (\tau=1),\\
    2^{(p^2+8)/4}, & p \text{ even } (\tau=2).
  \end{cases}
\]
\end{corollary}

\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{cccc}
\toprule
$p$ & $\tau(p)$ & orbits & capacity \\
\midrule
$2$ & $2$ & $3$ & $8$ \\
$3$ & $1$ & $3$ & $8$ \\
$4$ & $2$ & $6$ & $64$ \\
$5$ & $1$ & $7$ & $128$ \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

The coincidence at $p=2$ and $p=3$ is instructive: the small even torus has more
two-torsion, hence more singleton orbits, exactly compensating its smaller cell
count.

\section{Glide reflections}\label{sec:glide}

The remaining generator of a planar crystallographic group is the \emph{glide
reflection}: a reflection composed with a translation by half a period along the
mirror line. It is the unique kind of planar isometry that is a symmetry of no
point; musically it is the ``inverted answer displaced by half a bar''.

\begin{definition}[Toroidal glide]
For even $p$, define
$\gamma(t,n) := \bigl(t + \tfrac{p}{2},\,-n\bigr)$ on $\ZZ_p\times\ZZ_q$.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}
For even $p$, $\gamma^2 = \mathrm{id}$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
$\gamma^2(t,n) = (t + p/2 + p/2,\, n) = (t+p, n) = (t,n)$, using
$p/2+p/2 = p \equiv 0$ in $\ZZ_p$ (evenness enters here) and $-(-n)=n$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Fixed-point freeness]
For even $p\ge 2$, $\Fix(\gamma) = \emptyset$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
A fixed cell requires $t + p/2 = t$ in $\ZZ_p$, hence $p\mid p/2$; but
$0 < p/2 < p$, a contradiction.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Glide capacity]
For even $p$, the glide $\gamma$ has exactly $pq/2$ orbits on the $p\times q$
torus, all of size two, and the number of $\gamma$-invariant patterns is exactly
$2^{pq/2}$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
The Burnside sum is $pq + 0$ over a group of order two, so $m = pq/2$; apply
Theorem~\ref{thm:capacity}. All orbits have size two by freeness of the
nontrivial element.
\end{proof}

\begin{example}
$4\times 3$: $2^{6} = 64$. $2\times 2$: $2^{2} = 4$.
\end{example}

Among the crystallographic generators the glide has the cleanest price: it
halves the bit budget exactly, with no parity corrections, because it fixes
nothing.

\section{Monotonicity of capacity, sharply}\label{sec:monotone}

Let $H\le K\le G$. Every $H$-orbit is contained in a $K$-orbit, so there is a
canonical surjection $\alpha/H\twoheadrightarrow\alpha/K$.

\begin{theorem}[Antitonicity]
If $H\le K$ then $|\alpha/K|\le|\alpha/H|$ and hence
$|\Pat_K(\alpha)| \le |\Pat_H(\alpha)|$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Strictness]\label{thm:strict}
If $H \le K$ and there exist cells $a,b$ with $b\in K\cdot a$ but
$b\notin H\cdot a$, then $|\Pat_K(\alpha)| < |\Pat_H(\alpha)|$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
The canonical surjection sends the distinct classes $[a]_H \ne [b]_H$ to the
same $K$-class, so it is surjective and not injective; such a map of finite sets
strictly decreases cardinality, and $m\mapsto 2^m$ is strictly increasing.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Exact converse]\label{thm:converse}
If $H\le K$ and $b\in K\cdot a$ implies $b\in H\cdot a$ for all $a,b$, then the
canonical map is a bijection and $|\Pat_K(\alpha)| = |\Pat_H(\alpha)|$.
\end{theorem}

Together, Theorems~\ref{thm:strict} and \ref{thm:converse} identify the exact
condition for a capacity drop: capacity strictly falls if and only if the larger
group genuinely fuses orbits. Imposing a symmetry the smaller group already
realizes is free.

\begin{example}
On the four-beat, one-instrument grid, requiring invariance under the full shift
group leaves strictly fewer patterns ($2$) than requiring nothing ($2^4 = 16$),
witnessed by the cells $(0,0)$ and $(1,0)$, which the full shift group merges
and the trivial group does not.
\end{example}

\section{Every symmetry type is realized}\label{sec:realize}

\begin{theorem}[Realizability, translation action]
Let a group $G$ act on itself by left translation and let $\mathbf{1}_H$ be the
indicator pattern of a subgroup $H\le G$. Then $\Sym_G(\mathbf{1}_H) = H$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
($\supseteq$) If $g\in H$ then $ga\in H \iff a\in H$, so
$\mathbf{1}_H(ga) = \mathbf{1}_H(a)$ for all $a$. ($\subseteq$) If
$g\in\Sym_G(\mathbf{1}_H)$, evaluate at $a=1$: $\mathbf{1}_H(g) =
\mathbf{1}_H(1) = 1$, so $g\in H$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Realizability on a drum grid]
Let $C_p$ act on the $p\times q$ torus by time shifts, and for $H\le C_p$ set
$f_H(t,n) := 1$ if the shift by $t$ lies in $H$ and $0$ otherwise. Then
$\Sym_{C_p}(f_H) = H$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Containment of $H$ follows since $H$-membership of $t$ is unchanged by
multiplying by an element of $H$; the reverse containment evaluates at $(0,0)$.
\end{proof}

Consequently no symmetry type is missing: the whole subgroup lattice, from
trivial to total, is realized by explicit patterns. Moreover the symmetry group
of a pattern is strictly finer information than the ambient group, and the two
must not be conflated. The backbeat on a four-beat cycle (onsets at $t=0,2$) is
the canonical example: its symmetry group contains the half-bar shift, so it is
nontrivial, and omits the one-beat shift, so it is proper.

\section{Canons: a musical label with a complete numerical signature}
\label{sec:canon}

\begin{definition}[Canon]\label{def:canon}
A pattern $f$ on the $p\times q$ torus is a \emph{canon at time distance
$g\in\ZZ_p$} if $f(t+g,n) = f(t,n)$ for all $(t,n)$; equivalently the shift by
$g$ lies in $\Sym_{C_p}(f)$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}
$\ons(f) := |\{a : f(a) = 1\}|$.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}[Free actions divide invariant sets]\label{lem:divide}
If a finite group $\Gamma$ acts freely on a finite set $\alpha$ and
$S\subseteq\alpha$ is $\Gamma$-invariant, then $|\Gamma|$ divides $|S|$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
$\Gamma$ acts on $S$; every stabilizer is trivial, so every orbit has exactly
$|\Gamma|$ elements, and $S$ is the disjoint union of its orbits.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Canon divisibility]\label{thm:canondvd}
If $f$ is a canon at time distance $g$ on the $p\times q$ torus and $d$ is the
additive order of $g$ in $\ZZ_p$, then $d \mid \ons(f)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
The cyclic group generated by the shift by $g$ has order $d$ and acts freely
(Lemma~\ref{lem:free}); the onset set is invariant under it by
Definition~\ref{def:canon}. Apply Lemma~\ref{lem:divide}.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Fibers of the orbit map]\label{lem:fiber}
For a free action of a finite $\Gamma$ on a finite $\alpha$, every fiber of
$\alpha\to\alpha/\Gamma$ has exactly $|\Gamma|$ elements.
\end{lemma}

\begin{lemma}[Invariant subsets of every admissible size]\label{lem:invsub}
For a free action of a finite $\Gamma$ on a finite $\alpha$ with $M$ orbits, and
every $j\le M$, there is a $\Gamma$-invariant $S\subseteq\alpha$ with
$|S| = |\Gamma|\,j$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
Take $S$ to be the union of any $j$ orbits; it is invariant by construction and
each orbit contributes $|\Gamma|$ elements by Lemma~\ref{lem:fiber}.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Sharpness]\label{thm:sharp}
Let $d$ be the additive order of $g$ in $\ZZ_p$. For every $k$ with $d\mid k$
and $k\le pq$ there is a canon at time distance $g$ on the $p\times q$ torus
with exactly $k$ onsets.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Write $k = dj$. The shift group $\Gamma$ generated by $g$ has order $d$ and acts
freely, so it has $M = pq/d$ orbits, and $k\le pq$ gives $j\le M$. Take $S$ from
Lemma~\ref{lem:invsub} and let $f$ be its indicator: $S$ is shift-invariant, so
$f$ is a canon at distance $g$, and $\ons(f) = |S| = dj = k$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Complete onset spectrum]\label{thm:spectrum}
A natural number $k$ is the onset count of some canon at time distance $g$ on
the $p\times q$ torus if and only if $d\mid k$ and $k\le pq$, where
$d = \ord(g)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Necessity is Theorem~\ref{thm:canondvd} with the bound $\ons(f)\le pq$;
sufficiency is Theorem~\ref{thm:sharp}.
\end{proof}

\begin{example}
On the four-beat, one-instrument grid with $g=2$ (a half-bar canon), $d=2$, so
the achievable onset counts are exactly $\{0,2,4\}$. The backbeat, with onsets
at $t=0,2$, realizes $k=2$.
\end{example}

Theorem~\ref{thm:spectrum} is the model we advocate for musical vocabulary:
state the label as a predicate, derive a numerical consequence, and prove the
consequence is exactly right, so that the test neither over- nor under-rejects.

\section{Entropy: capacity as an information-theoretic ceiling}
\label{sec:entropy}

Write $\eta(x) := -x\ln x$ (with $\eta(0)=0$), and for a distribution $P$ on a
finite set $\beta$ let $H(P) := \frac{1}{\ln 2}\sum_{b}\eta(P(b))$ be its
Shannon entropy in bits.

\begin{theorem}[Maximum entropy]\label{thm:maxent}
For a probability distribution $P$ on a finite nonempty $\beta$,
$\sum_b \eta(P(b)) \le \ln|\beta|$, i.e.\ $H(P)\le\log_2|\beta|$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
$\eta$ is concave, so Jensen's inequality with uniform weights $1/n$,
$n=|\beta|$, gives $\frac1n\sum_b\eta(P(b)) \le \eta\bigl(\frac1n\sum_b
P(b)\bigr) = \eta(1/n) = \frac1n\ln n$; multiply by $n$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Equality case]\label{thm:eqcase}
Equality holds in Theorem~\ref{thm:maxent} if and only if $P$ is uniform.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
$\eta$ is strictly concave, and the equality case of Jensen's inequality for a
strictly concave function with strictly positive weights forces all the
arguments $P(b)$ to coincide; being a probability distribution, they all equal
$1/|\beta|$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Entropy ceiling for invariant patterns]\label{thm:ceiling}
Let $G$ act on a finite cell set with $m$ orbits and let $P$ be any probability
distribution on $\Pat_G(\alpha)$. Then $H(P)\le m$, with equality if and only if
$P$ is uniform.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
By Theorem~\ref{thm:capacity}, $\log_2|\Pat_G(\alpha)| = m$; apply
Theorems~\ref{thm:maxent} and \ref{thm:eqcase}.
\end{proof}

Theorem~\ref{thm:ceiling} is the precise sense in which the orbit count is the
information capacity of a style: no generative model producing only
$G$-invariant patterns can carry more than $m$ bits per pattern.

\begin{definition}[Binary entropy]
For $\theta\in[0,1]$,
$H_2(\theta) := \frac{\eta(\theta)+\eta(1-\theta)}{\ln 2}
 = -\theta\log_2\theta - (1-\theta)\log_2(1-\theta)$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}\label{thm:h2}
$H_2(\theta)\le 1$ for all $\theta\in[0,1]$, with equality iff $\theta=1/2$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Apply Theorems~\ref{thm:maxent} and \ref{thm:eqcase} to the two-point
distribution $(\theta,1-\theta)$ and divide by $\ln 2$.
\end{proof}

\begin{definition}[Product weight]
For a finite index set $\iota$ and $\theta\in[0,1]$, define on configurations
$x:\iota\to\{0,1\}$
\[
  w_\theta(x) := \prod_{i\in\iota}\theta^{\,x_i}(1-\theta)^{\,1-x_i}.
\]
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}[Normalization]\label{lem:norm}
$\sum_x w_\theta(x) = 1$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
Expand $\prod_i(\theta + (1-\theta)) = 1$ by distributivity: the terms of the
expansion are exactly the $w_\theta(x)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Entropy of a product weight]\label{thm:prodent}
$\displaystyle\sum_{x\in\{0,1\}^{\iota}}\eta\bigl(w_\theta(x)\bigr)
 = |\iota|\bigl(\eta(\theta)+\eta(1-\theta)\bigr)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Induct on $|\iota|$. Split a configuration on $n+1$ coordinates as a first bit
$b$ together with a configuration $y$ on the remaining $n$, so
$w_\theta(b,y) = c_b\,w_\theta(y)$ with $c_b\in\{\theta,1-\theta\}$. Use
$\eta(uv) = v\,\eta(u) + u\,\eta(v)$ with $u=c_b$, $v=w_\theta(y)$; summing over
$y$ and applying Lemma~\ref{lem:norm} and the inductive hypothesis gives
\[
  \sum_y \eta(w_\theta(b,y)) = \eta(c_b) + c_b\cdot n\bigl(\eta(\theta) +
  \eta(1-\theta)\bigr).
\]
Summing over the two values of $b$ and using $\theta + (1-\theta) = 1$ yields
$(n+1)(\eta(\theta)+\eta(1-\theta))$. The passage from $\{1,\dots,n\}$ to a
general finite index set is by transport along any bijection, under which
$w_\theta$ is invariant since the defining product is over all coordinates.
\end{proof}

\begin{definition}[Orbit-Bernoulli distribution]
Fix $\theta\in[0,1]$. Using the bijection
$\Pat_G(\alpha)\cong\{0,1\}^{\alpha/G}$ of Theorem~\ref{thm:capacity}, set
$P_\theta(f) := w_\theta(\text{orbit configuration of } f)$: switch each orbit
on independently with probability $\theta$. By Lemma~\ref{lem:norm} this is a
probability distribution.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Exact entropy of the orbit-Bernoulli model]\label{thm:obent}
$H(P_\theta) = m\,H_2(\theta)$, where $m$ is the number of orbits.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Transport the entropy sum along the bijection of Theorem~\ref{thm:capacity} (a
bijection permutes the summands), apply Theorem~\ref{thm:prodent} with
$\iota = \alpha/G$ so $|\iota| = m$, and divide by $\ln 2$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Per-orbit entropy deficit]\label{thm:deficit}
$m - H(P_\theta) = m\bigl(1 - H_2(\theta)\bigr)$. It is nonnegative for every
$\theta\in[0,1]$, and if $m>0$ it vanishes if and only if $\theta = 1/2$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
The identity is Theorem~\ref{thm:obent} rearranged; nonnegativity and the
equality case follow from Theorem~\ref{thm:h2} with $m\ge0$, cancelling $m$ when
$m>0$.
\end{proof}

\subsection{Interpretation: geometry times taste}

Theorem~\ref{thm:deficit} decouples structure from style:
\[
  \underbrace{m}_{\text{geometry}}\times
  \underbrace{\bigl(1-H_2(\theta)\bigr)}_{\text{taste}}
  \;=\; \text{total information lost to stylistic bias.}
\]
The orbit count $m$ is determined by the symmetry group and the grid, and
computed by Burnside's lemma from fixed-point data. The per-orbit deficit
$1-H_2(\theta)$ depends only on onset density and not at all on the geometry.
Representative values: $\theta=0.5$ costs $0$ bits per orbit; $\theta=0.3$ costs
$\approx 0.119$; $\theta=0.2$ costs $\approx 0.278$; $\theta=0.1$ costs
$\approx 0.531$; $\theta = 0.05$ costs $\approx 0.714$.

\section{Algorithms}

\paragraph{Algorithm A (Burnside capacity).}
Input a finite cell set and a finite list of group elements. For each $g$ count
$|\Fix(g)|$; sum and divide by $|G|$ to get $m$
(Theorem~\ref{thm:burncap}); return $(m, 2^m)$. Cost
$\Theta(|G|\cdot|\alpha|)$ evaluations of the action, with no orbit enumeration.

\paragraph{Algorithm B (orbit enumeration by union--find).}
Union each cell $a$ with $g\cdot a$ for each generator $g$; the resulting
classes are the orbits. Cost near-linear in $|\alpha|\cdot|\text{gens}|$. Useful
as a cross-check on Algorithm A and for exhibiting the orbits themselves.

\paragraph{Algorithm C (canon synthesis at a prescribed onset count).}
Given $p,q,g,k$: compute $d = p/\gcd(p,g)$; if $d\nmid k$ or $k>pq$, report
impossibility; otherwise set $j=k/d$, take the union of any $j$ orbits of the
shift group generated by $g$, and return its indicator. Cost $\Theta(pq)$.
Correctness of both branches is Theorem~\ref{thm:spectrum}.

\section{Applications}

\textbf{Style capacity budgeting.} Given a symmetry hypothesis about a
repertoire, Algorithm A returns an exact number of bits available in that
style --- a hard upper bound on the description length of any symmetric
generative model (Theorem~\ref{thm:ceiling}).

\textbf{Detecting over-constraint.} Theorems~\ref{thm:strict} and
\ref{thm:converse} quantify the marginal cost of each added symmetry: a symmetry
that costs nothing is redundant given the others.

\textbf{Falsification of structural labels.} Theorem~\ref{thm:spectrum} turns
``this is a canon at distance $g$'' into an arithmetic test on the onset count
and guarantees that the test is neither over- nor under-restrictive.

\textbf{Model calibration.} Theorem~\ref{thm:obent} gives a closed-form entropy
for a one-parameter family of models. Matching an empirical onset density to
$\theta$ predicts entropy $mH_2(\theta)$; a significant gap indicates dependence
between orbits that the product model does not capture.

\textbf{Grid design.} Corollary~\ref{cor:dichotomy} says which point-group
symmetries are even available on a given grid: quarter-turn structure requires a
square grid.

\section{Discussion and limitations}

The results are exact and unconditional, but their scope should be stated
plainly. \emph{No wallpaper classification is claimed}: we have not proved that
rhythmic patterns are classified by the seventeen planar crystallographic
groups, and Corollary~\ref{cor:dichotomy} indicates why the claim needs support.
\emph{Musical labels are hypotheses}: names such as ``round'' or ``twelve-bar
blues'' are not consequences of any classification above and should be treated
as conjectures until given predicates of their own. \emph{Corpus claims require
a protocol}: any distributional statement about real music depends on an
encoding of onset grids, a tolerance rule, a detection procedure and a null
model, none supplied here. \emph{The product model is a first approximation}:
real styles correlate orbits, and computing their deficits requires a richer
family than the orbit-Bernoulli one.

\section{Future directions}

\begin{enumerate}
\item Faithful wallpaper-group models: define planar Euclidean isometries,
  lattices, discreteness and compact quotient conditions, and only then attempt
  an exact seventeen-group classification.
\item Determine, for each $(p,q)$, the complete group of planar symmetries that
  descends, extending Corollary~\ref{cor:dichotomy} from the point group to the
  full space group with its glides.
\item Entropy beyond product models: compute deficits for Markov and
  pairwise-interaction models on the orbit set.
\item Evaluate the fixed-point sums for the full dihedral and space groups on
  the torus, obtaining closed-form capacities for every descending type.
\item Corpus methodology: specify a reproducible encoding, tolerance rules,
  detection algorithm and null model before testing distributional claims.
\item Give formal predicates for named structures beyond the canon, each with a
  derived numerical signature and a sharpness theorem in the style of
  Theorem~\ref{thm:spectrum}.
\end{enumerate}

\section{Conclusion}

The information content of a symmetry-constrained pattern space is exactly its
orbit count, in bits. Burnside's lemma computes that count from fixed-point
data, which for each crystallographic generator on a toroidal grid is a one-line
calculation, yielding four closed-form capacities: $2^q$ for full translation
invariance, $2^{(pq+\tau(p)\tau(q))/2}$ for point reflection,
$2^{(p^2+2\tau(p)+\tau(p)^2)/4}$ for quarter turns on a square torus, and
$2^{pq/2}$ for glides. Capacity is antitone in the symmetry group, strictly so
exactly when orbits are genuinely fused; every subgroup is realized as the
symmetry group of an explicit pattern; the canon label has a complete onset
spectrum; and every probabilistic model respecting the symmetry obeys an entropy
ceiling of $m$ bits, with the natural biased model falling short by exactly
$m(1-H_2(\theta))$ --- one fixed cost per orbit.

